steps.body:
A string is a sequence of characters, so you can loop over it like an array. `for...of` hands you each character as a one-letter string:

```javascript
for (const ch of "hey") {
  console.log(ch);
}
```

When you need the position too, use an index loop with `str[i]`, from `0` up to `str.length - 1`:

```javascript harness
const word = "hey";
{{code}}
```

```javascript
for (let i = 0; i < word.length; i++) {
  console.log(`${i}: ${word[i]}`);
}
```

Two tests cover most character questions:

- Set membership: `"aeiou".includes(ch)` is true when `ch` is one of those letters.
- Ranges: `ch >= "0" && ch <= "9"` is true for a digit. Comparing strings uses character codes, so `"A"` to `"Z"` and `"a"` to `"z"` work the same way.

**The gotcha:** these tests are case sensitive. `"aeiou".includes("A")` is false, so lowercase the string first when case should not matter. JavaScript has no separate char type: `ch` is just a string of length 1.

example.code:
const phrase = "Intro to JavaScript";
const address = "Room 404, floor 3";

let vowels = 0;
for (const ch of phrase.toLowerCase()) {
  if ("aeiou".includes(ch)) {
    vowels++;
  }
}

let digits = 0;
for (let i = 0; i < address.length; i++) {
  const ch = address[i];
  if (ch >= "0" && ch <= "9") {
    digits++;
  }
}

console.log(`Vowels: ${vowels}`);
console.log(`Digits: ${digits}`);

