practice.solution:
const title = "Hack University Rocks";

let upper = 0;
let spaces = 0;

for (const ch of title) {
  if (ch >= "A" && ch <= "Z") {
    upper++;
  }
}

for (let i = 0; i < title.length; i++) {
  if (title[i] === " ") {
    spaces++;
  }
}

console.log(`Uppercase: ${upper}`);
console.log(`Spaces: ${spaces}`);

steps.body:
Strings are immutable, so you never edit one in place. Instead, start with `""` and add the characters you want with `+=`:

```javascript
let kept = "";
for (const ch of "a-b-c") {
  if (ch !== "-") {
    kept += ch;
  }
}
console.log(kept); // abc
```

To reverse, walk the indexes backwards from `str.length - 1` down to `0`:

```javascript harness
const str = "stressed";
{{code}}
console.log(rev);
```

```javascript
let rev = "";
for (let i = str.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
  rev += str[i];
}
```

Strings have no `reverse()` method, but arrays do. `[...str]` spreads a string into an array of characters, so `[...str].reverse().join("")` reverses in one line. A palindrome reads the same both ways: lowercase it first, because `"R" === "r"` is false.

**The gotcha:** prefer `[...str]` over the `str.split("")` you saw in the last lesson. Both work for plain letters, but only spread keeps an emoji in one piece. JavaScript stores most emoji as two UTF-16 units, and `split("")` cuts between them.

example.code:
const word = "encyclopedia";
const dessert = "stressed";
const name = "Racecar";

let noVowels = "";
for (const ch of word) {
  if (!"aeiou".includes(ch)) {
    noVowels += ch;
  }
}

let loopReversed = "";
for (let i = dessert.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
  loopReversed += dessert[i];
}
const spreadReversed = [...dessert].reverse().join("");

const lower = name.toLowerCase();
const isPalindrome = lower === [...lower].reverse().join("");

console.log(`No vowels: ${noVowels}`);
console.log(`Loop reverse: ${loopReversed}`);
console.log(`Spread reverse: ${spreadReversed}`);
console.log(`${name} is a palindrome: ${isPalindrome}`);

